WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.016
Quantization stores model values at reduced precision to lower memory use and often improve practical speed.

00:00:06.016 --> 00:00:10.084
Labels such as Q4, Q5, and Q8 describe broad precision families,

00:00:10.084 --> 00:00:11.563
though exact methods vary.

00:00:11.563 --> 00:00:16.370
Lower precision fits smaller hardware but can affect output quality differently across models.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.068
Higher precision is not automatically better in an operational test.

00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:25.245
The referenced benchmark observed that Q8 did not always outperform Q4 for every model.

00:00:25.245 --> 00:00:28.573
A larger quantization may consume more memory, reduce headroom,

00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:32.271
and trigger slower offloading without delivering a meaningful quality gain.

00:00:32.271 --> 00:00:37.078
Image-token budgets and preprocessing settings affect how much visual detail reaches the model.

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:40.776
Too little visual capacity may lose small text or objects.

00:00:40.776 --> 00:00:46.692
More tokens increase memory and latency and may not help if the source image is poor.

00:00:46.692 --> 00:00:50.390
Batch size, prompt-processing settings, context length, GPU offload, thread count,

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.608
and cache choices also change performance.

00:00:52.608 --> 00:00:58.155
Compare models under documented settings rather than treating the model name as the whole configuration.

00:00:58.155 --> 00:01:00.004
Local runners such as llama.

00:01:00.004 --> 00:01:04.811
cpp, Ollama, LM Studio, and other tools may use different backends, model files,

00:01:04.811 --> 00:01:07.030
prompt templates, defaults, and vision adapters.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.988
Runtime version can alter speed, compatibility, and output.

00:01:09.988 --> 00:01:14.056
Test a small matrix of sensible settings, record memory and completion,

00:01:14.056 --> 00:01:16.644
and keep one reproducible configuration per candidate.

00:01:16.644 --> 00:01:21.081
Choose the lowest-cost setting that meets task quality and stability rather than

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:23.300
assuming the largest file is safest.
